{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MUa3wRVGXqodUbC8Ba59ZU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tia7yo1nZKRav2b6umypqB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty, framing, and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Half-point increments allowed. Acceptance rate ~21% for Main.\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Diversity Defend? A Framework for Analyzing Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation Panel Security\"\n\nThe paper studies security of multi-judge LLM evaluation panels against adversarial manipulation. It formalizes the distinction between BFT random-fault and adaptive adversary models, then empirically analyzes 455 three-judge panels from 15 models (5 API, 10 open-source) across 9 attacks on MMLU/ARC. Core thesis: panel security is regime-dependent — weak-model panels show a weakest-link effect (η_max dominates), strong-model panels benefit from diversity (K_eff dominates). Attack transferability is a second dimension. The diversity-capability tradeoff is an open-source artifact.\n\n## Full Paper Structure and Content\n\n**Abstract + Intro:** Frames the problem as challenging the BFT analogy for multi-judge panels. Claims: (1) adaptive adversary framework, (2) regime characterization (2D: capability tier × attack transferability), (3) copula/family-label methodological findings, (4) ecosystem-specific tradeoff.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):** Three short paragraphs — multi-judge evaluation, adversarial attacks on LLM judges, BFT. Very brief (~15 lines). All citation markers show as \"?\" (missing references).\n\n**Framework (Section 3):** Threat model with two models (random-fault, adaptive). Theorem 1: ASR_panel = Θ(η_max). K_eff metric from MI matrix.\n\n**Results (Section 5):** 10 tables across 7 subsections covering: regime-dependent weakest-link (Tables 1-2), attack transferability (Table 3), adaptive vs random-fault (Table 4), per-judge concentration (Table 5), capability-matched analysis (Tables 6-7), panel size sensitivity (Table 8), selection strategies (Tables 9-10).\n\n**Discussion (Section 6):** Unified regime framework, panel design implications, BFT analogy limitations, ecosystem dynamics.\n\n**Limitations:** Small API panel count, idealized adversary, only MC benchmarks.\n\n**Key numbers:** \n- η_max significant in 42% of conditions overall (5/12), K_eff in 33%\n- BFT contrast Cohen's d=0.22, p=0.08 (not significant overall)\n- Diversity-capability tradeoff: OS r=-0.72, API r=-0.05\n- Two-stage algorithm doesn't beat Strongest-K (p=0.32)\n- Median R²=0.22 (low explanatory power)\n- Paper is 6 pages (EMNLP allows 8)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the regime-dependent characterization genuinely novel? Is \"it depends\" an incremental insight? Does the adaptive adversary framework offer sufficient theoretical depth?\n2. **Related work adequacy**: Are key prior works cited? (Note: all citations show as \"?\" — this is a serious formatting issue). Is the positioning against prior work fair and complete?\n3. **Framing and narrative**: The paper was reframed from \"diversity is a trap\" to \"when diversity helps vs hurts.\" Is the nuanced thesis compelling, or does the hedged conclusion (\"it depends on regime\") reduce impact?\n4. **Contribution weight**: The paper lists 4 contributions. Are they individually substantial? Is the copula finding (Contribution 3) actually demonstrated in the paper? (I don't see copula analysis in the results sections.)\n5. **Writing quality**: Clarity, organization, density, figures (note: no figures mentioned in the paper body, only tables)\n\nGive scores for Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), and an Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point increments) with detailed justification.\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (numbered)\n### Weaknesses (numbered)\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing References (if any)\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5","run_in_background":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":30801,"cache_read_input_tokens":23768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":30801},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05705458-492a-44e3-b15f-334b33d72b35","uuid":"d987780c-30bf-42cc-84ec-229b626695a2"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aeb3c6d5cb9f095b8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tia7yo1nZKRav2b6umypqB","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty, framing, and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Half-point increments allowed. Acceptance rate ~21% for Main.\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Diversity Defend? A Framework for Analyzing Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation Panel Security\"\n\nThe paper studies security of multi-judge LLM evaluation panels against adversarial manipulation. It formalizes the distinction between BFT random-fault and adaptive adversary models, then empirically analyzes 455 three-judge panels from 15 models (5 API, 10 open-source) across 9 attacks on MMLU/ARC. Core thesis: panel security is regime-dependent — weak-model panels show a weakest-link effect (η_max dominates), strong-model panels benefit from diversity (K_eff dominates). Attack transferability is a second dimension. The diversity-capability tradeoff is an open-source artifact.\n\n## Full Paper Structure and Content\n\n**Abstract + Intro:** Frames the problem as challenging the BFT analogy for multi-judge panels. Claims: (1) adaptive adversary framework, (2) regime characterization (2D: capability tier × attack transferability), (3) copula/family-label methodological findings, (4) ecosystem-specific tradeoff.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):** Three short paragraphs — multi-judge evaluation, adversarial attacks on LLM judges, BFT. Very brief (~15 lines). All citation markers show as \"?\" (missing references).\n\n**Framework (Section 3):** Threat model with two models (random-fault, adaptive). Theorem 1: ASR_panel = Θ(η_max). K_eff metric from MI matrix.\n\n**Results (Section 5):** 10 tables across 7 subsections covering: regime-dependent weakest-link (Tables 1-2), attack transferability (Table 3), adaptive vs random-fault (Table 4), per-judge concentration (Table 5), capability-matched analysis (Tables 6-7), panel size sensitivity (Table 8), selection strategies (Tables 9-10).\n\n**Discussion (Section 6):** Unified regime framework, panel design implications, BFT analogy limitations, ecosystem dynamics.\n\n**Limitations:** Small API panel count, idealized adversary, only MC benchmarks.\n\n**Key numbers:** \n- η_max significant in 42% of conditions overall (5/12), K_eff in 33%\n- BFT contrast Cohen's d=0.22, p=0.08 (not significant overall)\n- Diversity-capability tradeoff: OS r=-0.72, API r=-0.05\n- Two-stage algorithm doesn't beat Strongest-K (p=0.32)\n- Median R²=0.22 (low explanatory power)\n- Paper is 6 pages (EMNLP allows 8)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the regime-dependent characterization genuinely novel? Is \"it depends\" an incremental insight? Does the adaptive adversary framework offer sufficient theoretical depth?\n2. **Related work adequacy**: Are key prior works cited? (Note: all citations show as \"?\" — this is a serious formatting issue). Is the positioning against prior work fair and complete?\n3. **Framing and narrative**: The paper was reframed from \"diversity is a trap\" to \"when diversity helps vs hurts.\" Is the nuanced thesis compelling, or does the hedged conclusion (\"it depends on regime\") reduce impact?\n4. **Contribution weight**: The paper lists 4 contributions. Are they individually substantial? Is the copula finding (Contribution 3) actually demonstrated in the paper? (I don't see copula analysis in the results sections.)\n5. **Writing quality**: Clarity, organization, density, figures (note: no figures mentioned in the paper body, only tables)\n\nGive scores for Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), and an Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point increments) with detailed justification.\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (numbered)\n### Weaknesses (numbered)\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing References (if any)\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5","uuid":"ad000905-2bc4-4a3f-ab10-bab45f273c40","session_id":"05705458-492a-44e3-b15f-334b33d72b35"}
